Let us draw out a picture and see what we really have.  [asy]
unitsize(2 cm);

pair A, B;

A = (1,1);
B = (-3/2,9/4);

real parab (real x) {
  return(x^2);
}

draw(graph(parab,-2,2));
draw((A + (-1,-2))--(A + (1,2)));
draw((A + (1,-0.5))--(A + (-3,1.5)));
draw(parab,B--(B + (0,1)),dashed);
draw(A--(A + (0,-1)),dashed);

dot("$A$", A, S);
dot("$B$", B, SW);
[/asy] 

Note that we are dealing here with the parabola $y = x^2.$  The tangent line to this parabola is (recall the derivative of $x^2$ is $2x$) $$y = 2(1) \cdot x + 1 - 2 = 2x - 1.$$  A line perpendicular to this will be $$y = -\frac{1}{2x} + b,$$ for some $b$.  But because this line passes through the point $(1,1)$ we have $$1 = -\frac{1}{2(1)} + b = - \frac{1}{2} +b,$$ so $b = \frac{3}{2}$.  Therefore the equation for our line is $$y = -\frac{1}{2}x+ \frac{3}{2}.$$  Finally, we wish to find where this intersects $x^2$, so we must have $$x^2 = -\frac{1}{2}x+ \frac{3}{2},$$ i.e. $$x^2 + \frac{1}{2}x - \frac{3}{2} = 0.$$  Note that our discriminant is $$\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^2 - 4 \cdot 1 \cdot \left(-\frac{3}{2}\right) = \frac{25}{4},$$ so our line intersects $y = x^2$ at $$x = \frac{-\left(\frac{1}{2}\right) \pm \sqrt{\frac{25}{4}}}{2} = \frac{-1 \pm 5}{4},$$ i.e. at the points $\left(-3/2, \frac{9}{4}\right)$ and $(-1,1)$.  But $A = (1, 1)$ by definition, thus $B = \left(-3/2, \frac{9}{4}\right)$.
<answer>[\left(-3/2,9/4\right)]</answer>

