 The cross product of two vectors is a vector perpendicular to the plane containing them. Its magnitude is equal to the area of the parallelogram that has the two vectors as sides. We can compute the cross product using the determinant of a matrix. Let's consider the vectors $\mathbf{a} = \begin{pmatrix} a_1 \\ a_2 \\ a_3 \end{pmatrix}$ and $\mathbf{b} = \begin{pmatrix} b_1 \\ b_2 \\ b_3 \end{pmatrix}$. The cross product $\mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{b}$ is given by the determinant of the matrix $\begin{pmatrix} \mathbf{i} & \mathbf{j} & \mathbf{k} \\ a_1 & a_2 & a_3 \\ b_1 & b_2 & b_3 \end{pmatrix}$, where $\mathbf{i}$, $\mathbf{j}$, and $\mathbf{k}$ are the unit vectors in the $x$, $y$, and $z$ directions, respectively. Expanding this determinant along the first row, we get:
\begin{align*}
\mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{b} &= \begin{vmatrix} \mathbf{i} & \mathbf{j} & \mathbf{k} \\ a_1 & a_2 & a_3 \\ b_1 & b_2 & b_3 \end{vmatrix} \\
&= \mathbf{i} \left(a_2 b_3 - a_3 b_2\right) - \mathbf{j} \left(a_1 b_3 - a_3 b_1\right) + \mathbf{k} \left(a_1 b_2 - a_2 b_1\right).
\end{align*}
Substituting the components of our vectors, we have:
\begin{align*}
\mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{b} &= \mathbf{i} \left((-1)(-5) - (2)(4)\right) - \mathbf{j} \left((1)(-5) - (2)(3)\right) + \mathbf{k} \left((1)(4) - (-1)(3)\right) \\
&= \mathbf{i} (-3) - \mathbf{j} (-11) + \mathbf{k} (7) \\
&= \begin{pmatrix} -3 \\ 11 \\ 7 \end{pmatrix}.
\end{align*}
However, this answer is not one of the options provided. Let's double-check our work:
\begin{align*}
\mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{b} &= \mathbf{i} \left((-1)(-5) - (2)(4)\right) - \mathbf{j} \left((1)(-5) - (2)(3)\right) + \mathbf{k} \left((1)(4) - (-1)(3)\right) \\
&= \mathbf{i} (-3) - \mathbf{j} (-11) + \mathbf{k} (7) \\
&= \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ 11 \\ 7 \end{pmatrix}.
\end{align*}
Thus, the correct cross product of the given vectors is $\boxed{\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ 11 \\ 7 \end{pmatrix}}$.

