Consider the square root of the form $\sqrt{a + b\sqrt{c}}$, where $a, b, c$ are integers. This can often be expressed in the form $\sqrt{x} + \sqrt{y}$, so let's try $\sqrt{15 - 6\sqrt{6}} = \sqrt{x} - \sqrt{y}$ and $\sqrt{15 + 6\sqrt{6}} = \sqrt{x} + \sqrt{y}$. When we square both sides, we get: 
\begin{align*}
(1) \phantom{到} 15 - 6\sqrt{6} &= x + y - 2\sqrt{xy} \\
(2) \phantom{到} 15 + 6\sqrt{6} &= x + y + 2\sqrt{xy}
\end{align*}
Adding these two equations, we get:
\begin{align*}
30 &= 2(x + y) \\
\Rightarrow x + y &= 15
\end{align*}
Subtracting equation (1) from equation (2), we get:
\begin{align*}
12\sqrt{6} &= 4\sqrt{xy} \\
\Rightarrow \sqrt{xy} &= 3\sqrt{6} \\
\Rightarrow xy &= 54
\end{align*}
We need to solve the system of equations $x + y = 15$ and $xy = 54$. Factoring $xy = 54$ and looking for pairs that sum to 15, we see that $x = 9$ and $y = 6$ work. Thus, we can write:
\begin{align*}
\sqrt{15 - 6\sqrt{6}} &= \sqrt{9} - \sqrt{6} = 3 - \sqrt{6} \\
\sqrt{15 + 6\sqrt{6}} &= \sqrt{9} + \sqrt{6} = 3 + \sqrt{6}
\end{align*}
<answer> Therefore, $\sqrt{15 - 6\sqrt{6}} + \sqrt{15 + 6\sqrt{6}} = 3 - \sqrt{6} + 3 + \sqrt{6} = \boxed{6}"/></answer>

